course: dictionary
lesson: 1053
title: Bossy and the words we choose
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Bossy is an informal word for someone who always tells other people what to do, in a way that annoys them. You can be bossy in a game, in group work, or at home. Being in charge is not the same as being bossy: a good leader asks, listens and shares the job.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «A bossy captain isn't a good one, and you are allowed to leave a game that has stopped being fun.» The sentence tells you that bossy behaviour makes a captain worse, not better, and that you do not have to keep playing when it stops being fun.
explain: Some people avoid harsh words. A report may say «a natural leader» instead of «bossy». This is a kind of spin, which means choosing nice-sounding words to make something unkind sound good. Read between the lines, and ask what the writer really means.
fact: Bossy is an informal word, so you will hear it in chatty speech and stories more than in formal writing.
fact: «Bossy» means always telling other people what to do, in an annoying way.
fact: «Bossy boots» is a teasing name for a bossy person.
fact: Bossier and bossiest are the comparative and superlative forms: «My sister is bossier than me, but my cousin is the bossiest of all.»
fact: Modal verbs change how bossy a sentence sounds: «You must tidy up» orders, but «You could tidy up» gently suggests.
exercise: «Ella always tells the team where to stand and won't let anyone else have a turn.» What does bossy mean here? | Quick at running | Very good at maths | *Always telling others what to do in an annoying way | Kind and gentle with everyone
exercise: A friend says, «Stop being such a bossy boots!» What does bossy boots mean? | A pair of boots for walking in the rain | A football player who scores lots of goals | *A teasing name for a bossy person | A kind and helpful teacher
exercise: «A bossy captain isn't a good one.» What does this tell you about a good leader? | A good leader wins every single game | *A good leader does not order people about in an annoying way | A good leader never speaks during a game | A good leader always shouts the loudest
test: Which sentence gives an order rather than a suggestion? | «You could tidy up.» | «You might like to tidy up.» | «Perhaps you may tidy up.» | *«You must tidy up.»
test: A teacher writes that a child is «a natural leader» in a report. Why might she choose these words? | Because bossy is a kind word | *To sound nicer, even if the child can be bossy | Because it has more letters than bossy | Because natural leader means the child is always lazy
exam: Complete the sentence: «My sister is the ___ of all three of us.» | bossy | bossily | *bossiest | bossier
